body:
How to expose a few operations of a CMMS, ERP or MES REST API to a Copilot Studio agent as tools without writing a connector by hand: upload an OpenAPI file, pick the endpoints, describe them well, authenticate and add them to the agent. The page behind the link (Microsoft Learn: extend your agent with tools from a REST API) was opened on 20 November 2026 and is marked prerelease documentation, so screens and limits can change. The OpenAPI file below is an invented example of a small work order API. Use it to practise, then replace it with the specification of your own system.

=== Prerequisites and version notes ===
- Maker credentials and a Copilot Studio license, as the page states.
- A copy of the OpenAPI specification and the authentication details of the API.
- REST API tools are created from an OpenAPI v2 specification (JSON). The page says a v3 file is translated to v2 automatically; check the result after the import.
- The page does not state limits for file size or number of operations. Keep the file small and remove endpoints you do not want to expose.
- The feature is in preview. Do not build a production dependency without checking its current status.
- Tools run in the context of a user connection; a data policy for connectors can block them.

=== Template: OpenAPI v2 file (example, fictional host) ===
{
 "swagger": "2.0",
 "info": {
  "title": "Work Order API",
  "description": "Example CMMS work order API used to demonstrate a REST API tool.",
  "version": "1.0.0"
 },
 "host": "cmms.example.com",
 "basePath": "/api",
 "schemes": [
  "https"
 ],
 "paths": {
  "/workorders": {
   "get": {
    "summary": "Find work orders",
    "description": "Finds work orders for an asset, optionally filtered by status.",
    "operationId": "FindWorkOrders",
    "produces": [
     "application/json"
    ],
    "parameters": [
     {
      "name": "assetId",
      "in": "query",
      "required": true,
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Asset or machine ID, for example PRESS-12."
     },
     {
      "name": "status",
      "in": "query",
      "required": false,
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Open, InProgress or Closed."
     },
     {
      "name": "top",
      "in": "query",
      "required": false,
      "type": "integer",
      "description": "Maximum number of results, default 10."
     }
    ],
    "responses": {
     "200": {
      "description": "List of work orders",
      "schema": {
       "type": "array",
       "items": {
        "$ref": "#/definitions/WorkOrder"
       }
      }
     }
    }
   }
  },
  "/workorders/{id}": {
   "get": {
    "summary": "Get one work order",
    "description": "Returns one work order by its number.",
    "operationId": "GetWorkOrder",
    "produces": [
     "application/json"
    ],
    "parameters": [
     {
      "name": "id",
      "in": "path",
      "required": true,
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Work order number, for example WO-5521."
     }
    ],
    "responses": {
     "200": {
      "description": "The work order",
      "schema": {
       "$ref": "#/definitions/WorkOrder"
      }
     }
    }
   }
  },
  "/defectdrafts": {
   "post": {
    "summary": "Create a defect draft",
    "description": "Creates a draft defect report that a planner must review. It does not release or close anything.",
    "operationId": "CreateDefectDraft",
    "consumes": [
     "application/json"
    ],
    "produces": [
     "application/json"
    ],
    "parameters": [
     {
      "name": "body",
      "in": "body",
      "required": true,
      "schema": {
       "$ref": "#/definitions/DefectDraft"
      }
     }
    ],
    "responses": {
     "201": {
      "description": "Draft created",
      "schema": {
       "$ref": "#/definitions/DefectDraft"
      }
     }
    }
   }
  }
 },
 "definitions": {
  "WorkOrder": {
   "type": "object",
   "properties": {
    "id": {
     "type": "string",
     "description": "Work order number."
    },
    "assetId": {
     "type": "string",
     "description": "Asset or machine ID."
    },
    "status": {
     "type": "string",
     "description": "Open, InProgress or Closed."
    },
    "summary": {
     "type": "string",
     "description": "Short description of the job."
    },
    "createdOn": {
     "type": "string",
     "description": "Creation date, ISO 8601."
    }
   }
  },
  "DefectDraft": {
   "type": "object",
   "required": [
    "assetId",
    "description"
   ],
   "properties": {
    "assetId": {
     "type": "string",
     "description": "Asset or machine ID."
    },
    "description": {
     "type": "string",
     "description": "What was observed, in plain words."
    },
    "reportedBy": {
     "type": "string",
     "description": "Role of the reporter, not a personal name."
    }
   }
  }
 }
}

=== Template: tool descriptions ===
The agent decides from the description when to call a tool, so write it with synonyms and specifics, as the page suggests. Weak: "A service to manage work orders." Better: "A maintenance system used to find, retrieve and display existing work orders for a machine or asset, including status and short description. It can also create a draft defect report for a planner to review. It cannot close, release or approve work orders." Do the same for each parameter; blank parameter descriptions must be filled in before the wizard lets you continue.

=== How to use ===
1. On the agent's Overview or Tools tab choose Add tool or Add a tool, then New tool, then REST API.
2. Upload the JSON file, check the details, select Next.
3. On API plugin details edit the Description (see above). Choose a solution or leave it blank; the wizard then creates one with the default publisher. Select Next.
4. On Authentication choose None, API key (parameter label, parameter name and location Header or Query) or the OAuth option with client ID, client secret, authorization URL, token URL, refresh URL and optional scope. Prefer OAuth so people use their own identity.
5. On the page that lists the endpoint and method combinations enable only FindWorkOrders and GetWorkOrder first. Add CreateDefectDraft later, after the read tools work.
6. For each tool set Tool name and Tool description, review the parameters, then review the tool and publish.
7. Select Create connection, return to Add tool, choose REST API, pick each tool, select a connection and choose Add and configure.
8. Open each tool on the Tools page: under Additional details turn on Ask the end user before running for CreateDefectDraft (the default is No). Under Inputs check the Fill using setting of every input; Dynamically fill with AI is the default.

=== Worked example (invented data) ===
A line lead asks: "What is open on PRESS-12?" The agent calls FindWorkOrders with assetId PRESS-12 and status Open and receives two items: WO-5521 (lubrication system alarm) and WO-5530 (guard sensor adjustment). Answer: two open jobs, numbers and summaries listed. Next the lead says: "Raise a defect: oil mist at the rear guard." The agent prepares CreateDefectDraft with assetId PRESS-12 and the description, shows the confirmation because Ask the end user before running is on, and only after approval creates the draft. The planner reviews it. Machine IDs, numbers and text are examples.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Importing the full vendor API with dozens of write operations and enabling all of them.
- Short descriptions that make two similar tools indistinguishable. The agent then picks one unpredictably.
- Passing a free-text "reportedBy" with a personal name. Use a role or the signed-in user from the connection if your policy allows it.
- Using a service account for all users so that the system cannot tell who asked.
- Forgetting that the wizard saves a solution; move it through your ALM process instead of editing in production.

=== Adapting it to your environment ===
Copy only the operations you need into the OpenAPI file and give each a plain-language summary. If the API returns large payloads, add a top parameter and a default. For aviation records keep release, deferral and sign-off operations out of the file; they stay in the maintenance system under the control of certifying staff. For a plant without a CMMS the same pattern works for a quality or downtime API.

=== Security and privacy ===
Do not paste API keys or client secrets into descriptions, instructions or chat. Create a dedicated API identity with the minimum role, and restrict the connection to the environment that needs it. Check data policies, data residency and logging. If the API returns employee data, clarify purpose, retention and works council approval before you enable the tool.
